<h2 id="apps-heading">Cooling Conveyor Applications</h2>
<h3>Bakery</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/">Bakery cooling</a> is the largest application for cooling conveyors. Bread exits the oven at 200+ F and must cool to 95-100 F before slicing and bagging. Cookies cool from 300 F to 80-90 F before wrapping. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/cookie-cracker/">Cookie and cracker lines</a> need gentle handling during cooling to prevent breakage.</p>
<h3>Confectionery</h3>
<p>Chocolate-enrobed products (bars, nuts, pretzels) pass through a cooling tunnel to set the chocolate coating. The tunnel maintains 55-65 F with controlled humidity to prevent bloom (white discoloration from fat or sugar crystal migration). Belt speed and tunnel length provide 5-15 minutes of cooling time. Cooling tunnels for confectionery use stainless steel mesh belts and <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary frame construction</a>.</p>
<h3>Meat and Poultry</h3>
<p>Cooked meat products must cool from cooking temperature (165+ F) to below 40 F within USDA-mandated time limits to prevent bacterial growth. Spiral coolers with refrigerated air handling achieve rapid cooling in a compact footprint. Stainless steel construction and IP69K-rated components handle the daily washdown required in meat plants.</p>
<h3>Industrial and Non-Food</h3>
<p>Cooling conveyors serve non-food applications including plastic part cooling after injection molding, metal part cooling after heat treatment, rubber product cooling after vulcanization, and glass cooling after forming. These applications use carbon steel mesh belts (no food-grade requirement) and ambient or forced-air cooling. Part weight and temperature determine belt construction and support structure.</p>
<h2 id="sizing-heading">Sizing a Cooling Conveyor</h2>
<p>Cooling conveyor sizing starts with required cooling time, the time needed to reduce product from inlet temperature to target temperature. Cooling time depends on product thermal properties (mass, specific heat, thermal conductivity, surface area), cooling medium temperature, and air velocity.</p>
<p>A simplified sizing approach:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Determine required cooling time</strong> from product testing or heat transfer calculations. For bakery products, empirical data from similar products is the most reliable source.</li>
<li><strong>Calculate belt length</strong> = belt speed (FPM) x cooling time (minutes). For spirals, this is the total belt path length inside the tower.</li>
<li><strong>Determine belt width</strong> from product width, number of lanes, and edge clearance. Minimum 2 inches of edge clearance per side.</li>
<li><strong>Calculate throughput</strong> = products per belt foot x belt speed (FPM). Verify that throughput meets production requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Add 15-20% safety factor</strong> for product variation, ambient temperature changes, and belt loading inefficiency.</li>
</ol>
<p>For budgeting, straight-line ambient cooling conveyors cost $$ per linear foot. Forced-air tunnels cost $$$ due to the enclosure, air handling, and controls. Spiral coolers cost $$$$ but deliver maximum cooling capacity per square foot of floor space. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for detailed cost factors.</p>
